<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><rss xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" version="2.0"><channel><title>India News | Latest India News Today &amp; Real-time News from India, India Breaking News</title><link>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india</link><description>India News Today: Get latest India news and breaking news from India on Times of India. TOI brings the top news headlines from India on Politics, Elections, Current Affairs and more</description><language>en-gb</language><lastBuildDate>2026-11-12T05:29:25+05:30</lastBuildDate><atom:link type="application/rss+xml" rel="self" href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/rssfeeds/-2128936835.cms"/><copyright>Copyright:(C) Thu, 12 Nov 2026 05:29:25 IST Bennett Coleman &amp; Co. Ltd, http://in.indiatimes.com/policyterms/1554651.cms</copyright><docs>http://syndication.indiatimes.com/</docs><image><title>India News | Latest India News Today &amp; Real-time News from India, India Breaking News</title><url>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/507610.cms</url><link>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india</link></image><item><title><![CDATA[Navi Mumbai India's cleanest city, Indore's domination ends]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/navi-mumbai-indias-cleanest-city-indores-domination-ends/articleshow/135748695.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135748695.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[For the 2025-26 Swachh Survekshan Awards, Navi Mumbai has claimed the esteemed title of the cleanest city. Following closely are Surat and Vijayawada, securing the second and third positions, respectively. Additionally, Rewa from Madhya Pradesh has been honored as the cleanest riverfront town this year. Significant mentions include Rudrapur as the cleanest Himalayan city, along with various award categories highlighting different city populations.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/navi-mumbai-indias-cleanest-city-indores-domination-ends/articleshow/135748695.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/navi-mumbai-indias-cleanest-city-indores-domination-ends/articleshow/135748695.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T02:55:54+05:30</pubDate><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135748773,imgsize-104242.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Collegium wants transfer of HC judges streamlined, random ways discarded]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/collegium-wants-transfer-of-hc-judges-streamlined-random-ways-discarded/articleshow/135748630.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135748630.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[The Supreme Court collegium is working to establish a systematic policy for transferring judges among high courts. Currently, transfers are largely subject to personal criteria, which the collegium aims to revise. This new policy emphasizes objective reasoning to streamline the transfer process, reduce personal biases, and enhance accountability regarding judges’ conduct and expertise, marking a pivotal evolution in judicial integrity management.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/collegium-wants-transfer-of-hc-judges-streamlined-random-ways-discarded/articleshow/135748630.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/collegium-wants-transfer-of-hc-judges-streamlined-random-ways-discarded/articleshow/135748630.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T02:44:43+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Dhananjay Mahapatra</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135748659,imgsize-124410.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[SC: Indians married here can't get divorced abroad]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/sc-indians-married-here-cant-get-divorced-abroad/articleshow/135748558.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135748558.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[In a landmark decision, the Supreme Court declared that divorces executed overseas, based on grounds not acknowledged under Indian law, are invalid. This ruling implies that individuals who remarry after such foreign divorces may risk prosecution for bigamy. The court also affirmed that marriages conducted according to Indian traditions hold validity, irrespective of any foreign citizenship.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/sc-indians-married-here-cant-get-divorced-abroad/articleshow/135748558.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/sc-indians-married-here-cant-get-divorced-abroad/articleshow/135748558.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T02:38:33+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Amit Anand Choudhary</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135748598,imgsize-95716.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Complete data could've raised average Diwali pollution level 49%]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/complete-data-couldve-raised-average-diwali-pollution-level-49/articleshow/135748336.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135748336.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[On Diwali night, the absence of PM2.5 data from monitoring stations led to distorted pollution readings in Delhi. Research by the Centre for Research on Energy and Clean Air indicated that pollution levels might have increased by as much as 49%. While the Central Pollution Control Board pointed out calibration limitations, readings from the Delhi Pollution Control Committee were inconsistent.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/complete-data-couldve-raised-average-diwali-pollution-level-49/articleshow/135748336.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/complete-data-couldve-raised-average-diwali-pollution-level-49/articleshow/135748336.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T02:03:33+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Priyangi Agarwal</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135748346,imgsize-49840.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Yuge Yugeen museum set to open doors to public next year; cabinet approves Rs 3,230 cr]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/yuge-yugeen-museum-set-to-open-doors-to-public-next-year-cabinet-approves-rs-3230-cr/articleshow/135748181.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135748181.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/yuge-yugeen-museum-set-to-open-doors-to-public-next-year-cabinet-approves-rs-3230-cr/articleshow/135748181.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/yuge-yugeen-museum-set-to-open-doors-to-public-next-year-cabinet-approves-rs-3230-cr/articleshow/135748181.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-12T01:22:08+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Dipak K Dash and Ambika Pandit</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135748186,imgsize-71722.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[NTA plans exam centres in every district]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/nta-plans-exam-centres-in-every-district/articleshow/135748060.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="marg